        exam_type: "local",
        year: 2013,
        number: 36,
        topic: "Equilibrium",
        question: String.raw`A saturated solution of manganese(II) carbonate ($M = 114.95$) contains $5.44 \times 10^{-5}$ g of \ce{MnCO3} per100 mL at $25\,^\circ\mathrm{C}$. What is its $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{sp}}$ at this temperature?`,
        question_image: false,
        answer: "C",
        answer_image: false,
        choices: {
            A: String.raw`$4.7 \times 10^{-6}$`,
            B: String.raw`$3.0 \times 10^{-9}$`,
            C: String.raw`$2.2 \times 10^{-11}$`,
            D: String.raw`$2.2 \times 10^{-13}$`
        },
        images: {
            image: ["/assets/local/2013_local_36.png"],
            question_image: ["/assets/local/2013_local_36_question.png"],
            subtext: null
        },
        solution: {
            text: String.raw`First, determine the concentration of \ce{MnCO3}. Each mole of \ce{MnCO3} dissociates into one mole of \ce{Mn^2+} and

\ce{CO3^2+} each (both with concentrations $x$), which are necessary for finding $K_{\mathrm{sp}}$.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\hline
\ce{MnCO3(s)} $\rightleftharpoons --\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ + \ce{CO3^2-} &  &  \\
\hline
$x$ = \ce{[MnCO3]} = $5.44 \times 10^{-5}$ g \ce{MnCO3} $\times \frac{1\text{ mol }\ce{MnCO3}}{114.95\text{ g }\ce{MnCO3}}$ &  & $1$ \\
 & $\times$ & 0.100~L = 4⋅7 $\times 10^{-6}$ M \ce{MnCO3} \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

To calculate $K_{\mathrm{sp}}$, we can use the equation below

Thus, the answer is

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lc}
\hline
 & $K_{\mathrm{sp}}$ = \ce{[Mn^2+][CO3^2-]} \\
\hline
 & $K_{\mathrm{sp}} = x^{2} = (4.7 \times 10^{-6})^{2} = 2.2 \times 10^{-11}$ \\
$C$ & . \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}`,
            image: ["/assets/local/2013_local_36_solution.png"]
        }
