        number: 52,
        topic: "Bonding/Molecular Structure",
        question: String.raw`When 1.0 mole of \ce{H2O2} decomposes to form \ce{H2O} and \ce{O2}, 103~kJ of energy is released. Given the bond energies below, what is the bond energy of the \ce{O-O} single bond in \ce{H2O2}?
\[
\begin{array}{lll}
\text{Bond} & \ce{H-O} & \ce{O=O} \\
\text{Bond Energy, }\mathrm{kJ}\cdot\mathrm{mol}^{-1} & 463 & 498
\end{array}
\]`,
        question_image: false,
        answer: "C",
        answer_image: false,
        choices: {
            A: String.raw`+395 $\mathrm{kJ}\cdot\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$`,
            B: String.raw`+249 $\mathrm{kJ}\cdot\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$`,
            C: String.raw`+146 $\mathrm{kJ}\cdot\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$`,
            D: String.raw`+103 $\mathrm{kJ}\cdot\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$`
        },
        images: {
            image: ["/assets/local/2014_local_52.png"],
            question_image: ["/assets/local/2014_local_52_question.png"],
            subtext: null
        },
        solution: {
            text: String.raw`When calculating $\Delta H$ using bond energies:

$\Delta H_{\mathrm{reaction}} = \Sigma$ (bonds in reactants) $-\Sigma$ (bonds in products)

This is the opposite of calculating $\Delta H$ from heats of formation. Bonds in the reactants are broken and require energy; bonds in the products are formed and release energy.

Thus, the correct answer is
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
 & \Delta H_{\mathrm{reaction}} = 1\ce{O-O} + \text{2 }\ce{H-O}\text{ −(2 }\ce{H-O} + \text{12 O−−O)} = \text{1 }\ce{O-O}\text{ -12 O−−O} \\
 & 103\text{ kJ/mol} = \text{1 }\ce{O-O}\text{ -12(498)} \\
 & \ce{O-O} = \text{146 kJ/mol} \\
C & \text{.}
\end{array}
\]`,
            image: ["/assets/local/2014_local_52_solution.png"]
        }
