        topic: "Bonding/Molecular Structure",
        question: "Which statement about bonding is correct?",
        question_image: false,
        answer: "A",
        answer_image: false,
        choices: {
            A: String.raw`A $\sigma$ bond has cylindrical symmetry about the bonding axis.`,
            B: String.raw`A $\pi$ bond is twice as strong as a $\sigma$ bond.`,
            C: String.raw`A double bond consists of two $\pi$ bonds.`,
            D: String.raw`A $\pi$ bond results from the sideways overlap of hybridized orbitals.`
        },
        images: {
            image: ["/assets/local/2015_local_51.png"],
            question_image: ["/assets/local/2015_local_51_question.png"],
            subtext: null
        },
        solution: {
            text: String.raw`Single covalent bonds consist of a $\sigma$ bond, where orbitals directly overlap. Double bonds consist of one $\sigma$

bond and one $\pi$ bond, and triple bonds consist of one $\sigma$ bond and two $\pi$ bonds; from this, C can be eliminated. $\pi$ bonds are formed through sideways overlap of $p$ orbitals, so D is out. $\sigma$ bonds are stronger than $\pi$ bonds, because they involve direct orbital overlap, so B is out. (Note: double bonds are stronger than single bonds in general, because they consist of a $\sigma$ bond and a $\pi$ bond, but they are not twice as strong, and $\pi$ bonds by themselves are weaker.) This

leaves us with $A$. Indeed, sigma bonds are cylindrically symmetric around the bonding axis.`,
            image: ["/assets/local/2015_local_51_solution.png"]
        }
