        solution: {
            text: String.raw`Since \ce{HCl} is a strong acid while \ce{CH3CH2NH2} is a weak base, we know the pH will be

less than 7 at equivalence point so we can immediately eliminate C and D. At the equivalence. point, the major compound will be \ce{CH3CH2NH3+}, which has the same mole number with the initial \ce{CH3CH2NH2}. We can calculate the number of moles of initial ethylamine, under the assumption of $1^{\mathrm{g}}_{\mathrm{mL}}$ as the density of the solution.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lc}
\hline
 & \textbf{g} \\
\hline
$n = 20$ mL $\times 1$ & mL $\times$ 10\% $\times 1$ mol45 g = 4⋅4 $\times 10^{-2}$ mol ethylamine \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Next, we need to calculate the volume of the \ce{HCl} added.
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
V = \text{4⋅4 }\times 10^{-2}\text{ mol} &  \\
0.3000\text{ mol/L }\times 1000\text{ mL} & = \text{147 mL} \\
\text{L} & 
\end{array}
\]
With the total volume of the solution at end point, we can calculate the concentration of the ethylamine.
\[
\begin{array}{lll}
n &  & 4.4 \times 10^{-2}\text{ mol} \\
c =V_{\mathrm{total}} & = & \text{(0.020 L} + \text{0.147 L)} = \text{0⋅26 M}
\end{array}
\]
With this, we can now set up an equilibrium in terms of the dissociation of the conjugate acid, and solve for \ce{[H+]} at the end point. We first need to write the dissociation.
\[ \ce{CH3CH2NH3+}\rightleftharpoons --\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2} + \ce{H+} \]
Upon reaching the endpoint, both of the products will increase in concentration by $x$, while the concentration of \ce{CH3CH2NH3+} decreases by $x$. The equilibrium concentration of \ce{CH3CH2NH3+} is pretty much 0.26~M. This is because $K_{\mathrm{a}} = 10^{-10.75}$ ≪1, so equilibrium is far to the left. Thus, we can set up an equation in terms of $K_{\mathrm{a}}$.
\[ K_{\mathrm{a}} = \ce{[H+][CH3CH2NH2]} \]
\[ \ce{[CH3CH2NH3+]} \]
\[ K_{\mathrm{a}} = 0.26\text{ −x }\approxx^{2}x^{2} \]
0.26

Solving for $x$, we get $x = 2.2 \times 10^{-6}$. With this, we also know that \ce{[H+]} = $x = 2.2 \times 10^{-6}$ M. To solve for the pH, we can just take the negative log base 10, shown below. This will get us the pH at the endpoint.

Thus, the range that the pH is closest to is
\[
\begin{array}{llll}
-\mathrm{log}_{10}[\ce{H^+}] = -\mathrm{log}_{10} 2.2 \times 10^{-6} = & 5.66 &  &  \\
\text{bromocresol green} & \text{, or} & B & \text{.}
\end{array}
\]`,
            image: ["/assets/local/2016_local_35_solution.png"]
        }
