        id: "2018_national-I_20",
        exam_type: "national-I",
        year: 2018,
        number: 20,
        topic: "Thermodynamics",
        question: String.raw`What is the standard heat of formation $(\Delta H^\circ_{\mathrm{f}})$ of gaseous ethylene, \ce{C2H4(g)}?
\begin{align*}
\ce{H2(g) + 1/2 O2(g) &-> H2O(g)} & \Delta H^\circ = -242\text{ kJ }\mathrm{mol}^{-1} \\
\ce{2 C(s,graphite) + 3 H2(g) + 1/2 O2(g) &-> C2H5OH(l)}
\end{align*}
\[ \Delta H^\circ = -278\text{ kJ }\mathrm{mol}^{-1} \]
\[ \ce{C2H4(g) + H2O(g) -> C2H5OH(l)} \qquad \Delta H^\circ = -88\text{ kJ }\mathrm{mol}^{-1} \]`,
        question_image: false,
        answer: "B",
        answer_image: false,
        choices: {
            A: String.raw`16~kJ $\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$`,
            B: String.raw`52~kJ $\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$`,
            C: String.raw`83~kJ $\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$`,
            D: String.raw`285~kJ $\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$`
        },
        images: {
            image: ["/assets/national-I/2018_national-I_20.png"],
            question_image: ["/assets/national-I/2018_national-I_20_question.png"],
            subtext: null
        },
        solution: {
            text: String.raw`The reaction for the formation of \ce{C2H4} is given below:
\[ \ce{2 C(s) + 2 H2(g) -> C2H4(g)} \]
$\Delta H^{◦}_{\mathrm{f}}$ for \ce{C2H4(g)} needs to be calculated using the data given. Using Hess’s Law, we can obtain the reaction for the formation of \ce{C2H4(g)} by adding the three equations below:
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
\ce{H2O(g)}\rightarrow \mathrm{H}_{2}(\mathrm{g})\text{ +*0 1/2 }\ce{O2(g)} & \Delta H^{◦}= 242\text{ kJ }\mathrm{mol}^{-1} \\
2\text{ }\ce{C(s)}\text{ +3 }\ce{H2(g)}\text{ +:2 }\mathrm{H}^{2}(\mathrm{g})^{1}/_{2}\ce{O2(g)}\rightarrow\text{ ((((((}\ce{C2H5OH(l)} & \Delta H^{◦}= -278\text{ kJ }\mathrm{mol}^{-1} \\
\text{((((((}\ce{C2H5OH(l) -> C2H4(g) + H2O(g)} & \Delta H^{◦}= 88\text{ kJ }\mathrm{mol}^{-1}
\end{array}
\]
\[ \ce{2 C(s) + 2 H2(g) -> C2H4(g)} \]
Adding the values of $\Delta H^{◦}$, we get the value of $\Delta H^{◦}\mathrm{for}$ formation of \ce{C2H4}, which is 52~kJ/mol.

Hence, the correct answer is $B$.`,
            image: ["/assets/national-I/2018_national-I_20_solution.png"]
        }
