        id: "2021_national-I_24",
        exam_type: "national-I",
        year: 2021,
        number: 24,
        topic: "Thermodynamics",
        question: String.raw`A reaction has $K_{\mathrm{eq}} = 0.10$ at 300~K and $K_{\mathrm{eq}} = 0.50$ at 330~K. Which statements about this reaction in this temperature range must be correct?

\begin{enumerate}
  \item $\Delta H^\circ_{\mathrm{rxn}} > 0$
  \item $\Delta S^\circ_{\mathrm{rxn}} > 0$
\end{enumerate}`,
        question_image: false,
        answer: "C",
        answer_image: false,
        choices: {
            A: "I only",
            B: "II only",
            C: "Both I and II",
            D: "Neither I nor II"
        },
        images: {
            image: ["/assets/national-I/2021_national-I_24.png"],
            question_image: ["/assets/national-I/2021_national-I_24_question.png"],
            subtext: null
        },
        solution: {
            text: String.raw`Based on the information provided, $K_{\mathrm{eq}}$ increases with temperature for the reaction. According the van’t Hoff equation listed below, the forward reaction must be endothermic, so the $\Delta H_{\mathrm{r}}^\circ > 0$.

lnK= $-^{\Delta H}_{\mathrm{R}}^{\mathrm{ro}}\times^{1}_{\mathrm{T}}+$ C, where $C$ is a constant.

Difficulty: Hard

Learning Objectives: Le

Chatelier Principle; van’t

Hoff equation; relationship
\[ \text{between }\Delta G^\circ\text{ and the }K \]
You may get the same conclusion using the Le Chatelier Principle. By using the two $K_{\mathrm{eq}}$ values at different temperatures, we can calculate the $\Delta H_{\mathrm{r}}^\circ$:

8 / Annotated Solutions for \ce{USNCO} National Exam 2021 – Part I
\[
\begin{array}{llll}
\text{ln }\ce{K2} & = -\Delta H^{\mathrm{ro}} & \times\text{ (1} & \text{-1)} \\
\ce{K1} & \text{R} & \mathrm{T}_{2} & \mathrm{T}_{1}
\end{array}
\]
It is assumed that the $\Delta H_{\mathrm{r}}^\circ$ and $\Delta S_{\mathrm{r}}^\circ$ don’t change with temperature.
\[
\begin{array}{lll}
 & \Delta H_{\mathrm{r}}^\circ=\text{ −ln (0.50/0.10) }\times 8.314 \times 330 \times\text{ 300/(300 }-330) = 44 \times 10^{3}\text{ J/mol} &  \\
 & \text{Furthermore, we can use }\Delta G^\circ = \Delta H^\circ - T\Delta S^\circ = \text{– RT lnK to calculate the }\Delta S_{\mathrm{r}}^\circ\text{ at either temperature:} &  \\
^\circ= \Delta H^{\mathrm{ro}}+\text{ RTlnK} & = 44 \times 10^{3} + 8.314 \times 300 \times\text{ ln (0.10)} & = \text{128 J }\cdot \mathrm{mol}^{-1} \cdot \mathrm{K}^{-1} \\
\Delta S_{\mathrm{r}} &  &  \\
\text{T} & 300 &  \\
\text{Thus, both I and II are correct, the answer is C.} &  & 
\end{array}
\]`,
            image: ["/assets/national-I/2021_national-I_24_solution_1.png", "/assets/national-I/2021_national-I_24_solution_2.png"]
        }
