        year: 2021,
        number: 35,
        topic: "Equilibrium",
        question: "The graph shows the logarithm of the molar solubility $S$ of an ionic solid as a function of pH. Which solid is it?",
        question_image: true,
        answer: "D",
        answer_image: false,
        choices: {
            A: String.raw`\ce{Ag2C2O4}`,
            B: String.raw`\ce{AlPO4}`,
            C: String.raw`\ce{SrSO4}`,
            D: String.raw`\ce{BaF2}`
        },
        images: {
            image: ["/assets/national-I/2021_national-I_35.png"],
            question_image: ["/assets/national-I/2021_national-I_35_question.png"],
            subtext: null
        },
        solution: {
            text: String.raw`Difficulty: Super Hard Learning Objectives: solubility vs pH

$S = [\mathrm{M}^{n+}]$ for B, C, and D, while $S = 1/2$ \ce{[Ag+]} for A.

All four precipitates are made by cation and conjugate base of a weak acid (HA). Therefore, higher

pH shifts the dissociation equilibrium of HA to the side with the conjugate base, $\mathrm{A}^{-}$.

As $K_{\mathrm{sp}}$ is a constant, higher $[\mathrm{A}^{-}]$ decreases the cation concentration, resulting in a smaller molar

solubility $S$, which is consistent with the graph, log $S$ decreases with pH linearly first, and then reaches

a plateau region. The plateau region is caused by the steady $[\mathrm{A}^{-}]$ when pretty much all the acid is

deprotonated at high pH. Thus, by analyzing the pH of the plateau region, we may get some sense of

the $\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}$ of the acid. According to the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation, when pH = $\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{a}} + 2$, the

conjugate base, $\mathrm{A}^{-}$ is the dominate form in the solution ([$[\mathrm{A}^{-}]/[\mathrm{HA}] = 100$]) and its concentration doesn’t increase significantly with pH anymore. So based on the pH where the curve starts to level off, we may read the $\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}$ of each conjugate acid. So, we can eliminate [B] \ce{(AlPO4)} as \ce{PO4^3-} is such a strong base that changes in solubility would be observed up to a very high pH. Similarly, we can also eliminate [C], \ce{SrSO4}, as \ce{SO4^2-} has a much larger $K_{\mathrm{a}} (\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}$ of \ce{HSO4-} $\approx$ 2, the first dissociation of \ce{H2SO4} is complete). The extrapolations of the descending line and the flat asymptote intersect at the $\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}$, which is shown the figure below. That leaves us with \ce{AgC2O4} and $\mathrm{BaF}_{2,}$ which have similar $\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}\mathrm{s}$. The next level of discrimination is based on whether the acid is monoprotic or diprotic. This would not affect the appearance of the plateau—in all cases, there is only one leveling off point, where the fully deprotonated species becomes the dominant species in solution. What you would see with a diprotic acid such as oxalate is a “kink” in the linear descent region as the solubility equilibria go from involving two protons (steeper slope at lower pH) to involving only one proton (shallower slope at moderate pH) before leveling off where no protons are involved in the solubility equilibria (zero slope at sufficiently high pH). The figure above is a plot of log $S$ of \ce{CaCO3} vs pH with the extrapolation lines to show you the location of $\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{a}1}$ and $\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{a}2}$, as well as the “kink” with different slopes. It is worthy to point out that the extrapolation of two descending lines intersect at $\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{a}1} = 6.37$, while the extrapolations of the descending line and the flat asymptote intersect at the $\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{a}2} = 10.25$. Thus, we can eliminate the option [A] \ce{AgC2O4} with the conjugate base of a diprotic acid as we clearly see there is only one slope in the figure, which is consistent with the monoprotic acid option D \ce{[BaF2]}.

Annotated Solutions for \ce{USNCO} National Exam 2021 – Part I / 13

A comparison between the plot for \ce{BaF2} and \ce{Ag2C2O4} (for the latter, $\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{a}1} = 1.23$, $\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{a}2} = 4.19$, $K_{\mathrm{sp}} = 5.4\times10^{-12}$ was used) is attached for your reference.

Special thanks to Dr. Seth Brown for his insightful contributions to the annotations of this question!

Difficulty: Hard`,
            image: ["/assets/national-I/2021_national-I_35_solution_1.png", "/assets/national-I/2021_national-I_35_solution_2.png"]
        }
