        exam_type: "national-I",
        year: 2021,
        number: 39,
        topic: "Oxidation - Reduction",
        question: String.raw`A new electrode material is being tested for its ability to selectively reduce oxygen gas to hydrogen peroxide. After running an electrolytic cell using this electrode for 110.0 minutes at a current of 0.150 A, the solution in the cathode compartment is analyzed and is found to contain $2.00 \times 10^{-3}$ mol \ce{H2O2}. What is the percent yield of \ce{H2O2} based on the current passed through the cell (the faradaic yield)?`,
        question_image: false,
        answer: "B",
        answer_image: false,
        choices: {
            A: String.raw`19.5\%`,
            B: String.raw`39.0\%`,
            C: String.raw`78.0\%`,
            D: String.raw`100\%`
        },
        images: {
            image: ["/assets/national-I/2021_national-I_39.png"],
            question_image: ["/assets/national-I/2021_national-I_39_question.png"],
            subtext: null
        },
        solution: {
            text: String.raw`The percent yield of \ce{H2O2} can be calculated using the number of electrons to reduce oxygen gas to \ce{H2O2} divided by the total electron number. As 2 moles of electrons are needed to reduce oxygen gas to 1 mole \ce{H2O2}, we can calculate the percent yield as follows:
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{yield\%} = 2.00 \times 10^{-3}\text{ mol }\times 2\mathrm{e}^{-}/\mathrm{mol} & \times\text{ 100\%} = \text{39.0\%} \\
\frac{110.0 \times 60\text{ s }\times 0.150\text{ A}}{96500\text{ C/mol}} & 
\end{array}
\]
where $F = 96500$ C/mol is the Faraday’s constant, meaning the charge of 1 mole of electrons. Thus, the answer is B.

Difficulty: Hard

Learning Objectives:

electrolysis; Faraday’s law`,
            image: ["/assets/national-I/2021_national-I_39_solution.png"]
        }
